## Finding 1 (FAIL, fixable): the fixed nightly job uses the default warehouse, not the user's custom home

What the plan says: "Users whose stockroom home, `XDG_DATA_HOME`, `HOME` or `~/.local/bin` path holds a space or `'` … Their nightly job starts working." Its main example is `~/Library/Application Support` on macOS.

What happens with the planned code:

- `cron_install` and `launchd_install` bake the log path from the install-time `home_dir()`. That path honors `STOCKROOM_HOME` and `XDG_DATA_HOME`.
- The entry carries only `PATH`. cron and launchd pass neither variable. So at 03:30, `stockroom ingest` and `stockroom embed` resolve `~/.local/share/stockroom`.
- Probe (`/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mobaxterm-worktrees-Texarkanine-stockroom-stockroom-claudeboarding/3b628427-7950-4f51-90b9-cfcc5ad0911b/scratchpad/preflight-r4/scripts/custom_home_probe.py`, run on the planned `schedule.py` in a scratch copy):
    - Setup: `STOCKROOM_HOME`, and separately `XDG_DATA_HOME`, set to `<home>/Library/Application Support`.
    - Result: both entries exit 0, and the log lands in the custom home.
    - But the fake shim asks the engine's own `resolve_home()` which home it would use, and under the scheduler's environment the answer is `<home>/.local/share/stockroom` (source `default`).

Why this matters for this round:

- A space in the path almost always comes from `STOCKROOM_HOME` or `XDG_DATA_HOME`. Linux and macOS user names cannot hold a space, so `HOME` and `~/.local/bin` rarely do.
- So for most of the named group, the plan turns a job that fails into one that silently ingests and embeds into a second warehouse. On the first night that is a full ingest, plus an embed of all history.
- Meanwhile the real warehouse goes stale, and the log in the real home reports success. That signal misleads more than today's missing log does.
- The bug is not new. Every custom-home user has it today, with plain paths too. This round extends it to more users, and its claim is false for them.

Known fixes. The planner picks one with the operator:

- **(a) Carry the resolved home in the entry.**
    - cron: `PATH=<q> STOCKROOM_HOME=<q home> /bin/sh -c <q payload>`.
    - launchd: `EnvironmentVariables: {PATH, STOCKROOM_HOME}`.
    - Test: extend the Unit 1 fake shim to print `${STOCKROOM_HOME-<unset>}`, and assert that it equals the install home for all three folders. It is red today for all three, plain included. Add a mutant that drops the variable.
    - Plain entries then change bytes. Re-state the "byte for byte" claim and the status output. The existing entry tests use substring checks; confirm `test_install_on_empty_crontab_writes_exactly_the_managed_block` stays green.
    - Decide whether `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and the ingest-root overrides (`STOCKROOM_CURSOR_ROOT`, `STOCKROOM_CURSOR_CHATS_ROOT`, `STOCKROOM_CLAUDE_ROOT`) ride along. The scheduler drops them the same way.
- **(b) Re-scope, with operator sign-off.**
    - Keep Unit 1 as planned, and remove the "nightly job starts working" claim for custom homes.
    - List the custom-home bug among the out-of-scope items and in the PR #142 text.

Either way, existing entries are never re-rendered (advisory 1). That matters more under (a).

